  ✓ CONFIRMED: Zero-slippage reward liquidation enables sandwich-based pric
    Justification: Upon reviewing CurveSpell.closePositionFarm in 
blueberry-core/contracts/spell/CurveSpell.sol, I confirmed the zero-slippage 
reward liquidation vulnerability. After harvesting rewards through 
_doCutRewardsFee(CRV), the function approves the caller-supplied swapRouter and 
invokes swapExactTokensForTokens with the entire reward balance, amountOutMin 
set to 0, the caller-supplied swapPath, and no oracle or execution-price 
validation. Consequently, an attacker can manipulate the relevant Uniswap V2 
pool before this transaction executes, causing the reward-to-debt-token swap to 
receive materially less value, then reverse the manipulation afterward. The 
function also does not validate that the router, path endpoints, intermediate 
tokens, or liquidity sources are protocol-approved. Although subsequent Curve 
removal and repayment logic may cause the transaction to revert if insufficient 
debt tokens are received, a successful adverse swap can still impose value loss 
where the remaining flow succeeds; moreover, arbitrary routers introduce 
additional trust and token-transfer risks. The supplied oracle contracts do not 
protect this swap, as neither is consulted by closePositionFarm.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: PSwapLib forwards caller-controlled Paraswap slippage withou
    Justification: Upon reviewing PSwapLib.megaSwap and 
ShortLongSpell.closePosition/_withdraw, I confirmed that the spell performs no 
independent slippage or minimum-output validation for withdrawals. closePosition
only verifies that swapData.fromToken matches the strategy’s underlying token, 
then passes the entire caller-controlled MegaSwapSellData to _withdraw. 
_withdraw checks param.sellSlippage against the configured maximum, but never 
relates that value to swapData.toAmount, expectedAmount, the withdrawn amount, 
or an oracle-derived valuation. PSwapLib merely approves fromAmount and invokes 
the unprotected IParaswap.megaSwap entry point. Although Paraswap may enforce 
the supplied toAmount as a minimum at execution, that minimum itself is selected
by the caller and can be set near zero; expectedAmount is not checked during 
withdrawal, unlike _deposit, which explicitly compares the received balance 
against swapData.expectedAmount. Consequently, a caller can authorize a 
materially underpriced route or near-zero output, and the protocol has no local 
protection against it. The precise impact and whether an attacker can profit 
depend on caller authorization and broader position-management logic, but the 
stated missing validation is present.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: AggregatorOracle can return flash-loan-manipulated Uniswap V
    Justification: Upon reviewing AggregatorOracle.getPrice, 
UniswapV2Oracle.getPrice, and CoreOracle._getPrice, I confirmed that the 
implementation permits a manipulable reserve-based oracle path. 
UniswapV2Oracle.getPrice reads pool.getReserves() directly and uses those 
current reserves in its fair LP pricing formula, without any TWAP, 
observation-period, liquidity-depth, or freshness validation. 
AggregatorOracle._setPrimarySources permits up to three arbitrary sources, does 
not require a minimum source count, does not verify source independence, and 
getPrice explicitly returns a single valid source without applying deviation 
checks. Consequently, if an administrator configures UniswapV2Oracle as the sole
source, or configures correlated sources, a transaction that changes the pair 
state before the oracle call can cause the aggregator to return a transient 
valuation; CoreOracle then accepts any nonzero result from that route. The 
deviation logic only compares returned values and cannot detect synchronized 
manipulation or protect a single-source configuration. The supplied code does 
not show actual deployment routes or downstream liquidation logic, but the 
exploitable oracle design and missing safeguards are clearly present.
  ? UNCERTAIN: AggregatorOracle can accept flash-loan-manipulated Balancer 
    Note: After analyzing AggregatorOracle.getPrice, 
BalancerPairOracle.getPrice, and BalancerPairOracle.computeFairReserves, I 
cannot definitively confirm or reject the proposed flash-loan valuation attack 
from the supplied code alone. AggregatorOracle permits a single valid source to 
determine the price without deviation validation, and its multi-source checks do
not verify source independence; therefore, a configured BalancerPairOracle could
be trusted without an effective quorum. BalancerPairOracle also reads live 
balances through IBalancerVault.getPoolTokens and has no explicit TWAP, 
liquidity, or price-impact guard. However, it does not simply value the LP token
at the manipulated spot ratio: computeFairReserves uses the pool invariant, 
normalized weights, and base prices to derive fair reserves, which may 
substantially limit the effect of a temporary swap and requires demonstrating 
that the invariant or integer arithmetic can be profitably manipulated. The 
provided code also omits route configuration, BaseOracle implementation, and the
exact Balancer pool behavior during composed transactions. Those details are 
necessary to establish a concrete exploitable path and impact.
  ✗ REJECTED: UniswapV3AdapterOracle exposes flash-manipulable Uniswap V3 
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged flash-manipulable spot-price 
vulnerability in UniswapV3AdapterOracle.getPrice, I determined that the 
hypothesis is a false positive as characterized. The adapter does not read the 
current Uniswap V3 slot0 price. Instead, it obtains timeGaps, rejects a zero 
interval with NO_MEAN, and passes the configured secondsAgo value to 
UniV3WrappedLibMockup.consult(stablePool, secondsAgo), returning an 
arithmeticMeanTick that is then quoted through getQuoteAtTick. 
BaseAdapter.setTimeGap also constrains configured intervals between 
protocol-defined minimum and maximum values. Consequently, a price movement 
executed and reversed within one transaction should not immediately alter the 
consulted historical mean, unlike a spot oracle. CoreOracle merely routes the 
adapter result, and BlueBerryBank consumes that result through 
getWrappedTokenValue and getTokenValue without introducing a separate spot read.
The code does lack explicit liquidity checks and an independent deviation bound,
so prolonged manipulation or weak operational configuration may warrant review, 
but those omissions do not establish the claimed single-transaction 
flash-manipulation path.
  ? UNCERTAIN: Convex collateral valuation can be inflated through Curve vi
    Note: After analyzing CurveOracle._getPoolInfo and getPrice, 
ConvexSpell.openPositionFarm, and BlueBerryBank.getPositionValue, I found that 
Curve LP valuation directly consumes the current Curve registry virtual price 
through get_virtual_price_from_lp_token and calculates value as the minimum 
underlying token price multiplied by that spot value. The reviewed code contains
no freshness validation, bounded-change check, independent valuation, or TWAP 
comparison before the result is used by collateral and position-risk 
calculations. However, the source provided does not include the concrete Curve 
pool implementations, BaseOracle behavior, wrapped Convex-token valuation 
registration, or deployed configuration needed to establish that an attacker can
materially and temporarily inflate virtual price. In standard Curve 
implementations, virtual price is generally an accounting value that changes 
gradually with fees and is not ordinarily manipulated like a spot swap price, 
although pool-specific behaviors or donation/accounting mechanisms could alter 
that conclusion. Therefore, the code demonstrates an oracle robustness 
deficiency and a potentially unsafe trust boundary, but not a conclusively 
exploitable borrowing attack from the supplied evidence alone.
  ✗ REJECTED: Curve LP collateral in CurveSpell can be overvalued through 
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged manipulable Curve virtual-price valuation
in CurveOracle._getPoolInfo and getPrice, together with 
CurveSpell.openPositionFarm and BlueBerryBank.getPositionValue, I determined 
that the hypothesis is not demonstrated as an exploitable vulnerability by the 
supplied code. CurveOracle does rely on the Curve registry’s virtual price and 
multiplies it by the minimum price of the pool’s underlying tokens, without an 
explicit deviation bound or independent NAV check. However, the code does not 
show that an unprivileged attacker can arbitrarily inflate the canonical Curve 
pool virtual price: the registry and address provider resolve protocol-managed 
pools, while virtual price is an accounting metric rather than a directly 
supplied spot price. CurveSpell also validates strategy existence, collateral 
type, maximum LTV, and maximum position size before finalizing a position, and 
BlueBerryBank enforces the resulting risk checks. An anomalous or compromised 
Curve pool could still create valuation risk, but proving this attack requires 
pool-specific implementation and configuration details demonstrating a feasible 
virtual-price inflation mechanism. Therefore, the stated manipulation path is 
speculative rather than confirmed.
  ✗ REJECTED: HardVault share-price manipulation through unsolicited asset
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged share-price manipulation in 
HardVault.deposit and HardVault.withdraw, I determined this is a false positive.
The deposit function records the token balance before and after safeTransferFrom
and mints exactly the balance delta, so unsolicited tokens transferred before a 
user deposit are excluded from that deposit's minted amount rather than used in 
an exchange-rate calculation. More importantly, withdraw does not calculate 
redemption value from the vault's current token balance or total shares: it 
burns the caller's ERC1155 balance for the token ID and passes the same 
shareAmount to FeeManager.doCutVaultWithdrawFee, after which only the 
fee-adjusted amount is transferred. Consequently, a direct donation cannot 
dilute a victim's shares, and an attacker cannot redeem donated assets merely by
holding shares. The donation may remain stranded in the vault and could affect 
solvency or accounting expectations, but the described theft path does not 
exist. No virtual-share mechanism is needed to prevent the stated attack because
this implementation uses one-to-one deposit shares and withdrawals.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: SoftVault Compound exchange-rate inflation can overvalue col
    Justification: Upon reviewing SoftVault.deposit, BlueBerryBank.lend, and 
BlueBerryBank.getIsolatedCollateralValue, I confirmed a spot exchange-rate 
manipulation vulnerability in the collateral valuation path. SoftVault issues 
shares equal to the cToken balance increase returned by Compound mint, while 
getIsolatedCollateralValue converts those shares to underlying using 
ICErc20(bToken).exchangeRateStored() multiplied by pos.underlyingVaultShare and 
divided by PRICE_PRECISION. This value is then passed directly to 
oracle.getTokenValue without any bound, freshness, historical-rate, deviation, 
or same-transaction protection. An attacker can acquire SoftVault shares, 
manipulate the Compound market’s cash balance and exchange rate by supplying or 
directly transferring underlying to the cToken contract, and then invoke a 
borrowing path that relies on the inflated isolated collateral value. The bank’s
only relevant control is the liquidation-threshold check in execute, which 
itself uses the same manipulated valuation and therefore does not prevent 
over-borrowing. Although exact profitability depends on market liquidity, share 
ownership, and Compound implementation details, the code clearly exposes 
collateral valuation to a mutable spot exchange rate and provides no mitigation 
in the reviewed components.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Aura reward liquidation may use manipulable Uniswap spot pri
    Justification: Upon reviewing AuraSpell.closePositionFarm and 
WAuraPools.burn, I confirmed that the reward liquidation path performs an 
unprotected Uniswap V2-style swap. After burning the position and receiving 
rewardTokens, closePositionFarm calls 
swapRouter.swapExactTokensForTokens(rewards, 0, swapPath, address(this), 
type(uint256).max), explicitly setting amountOutMin to zero and using an 
effectively unlimited deadline. The caller also supplies the router and each 
swap path, while no TWAP, oracle quote, price-impact limit, or deviation check 
is performed before or after execution. Consequently, an attacker able to 
influence the selected Uniswap pool can sandwich or otherwise distort reserves 
immediately before the transaction, causing rewards to be exchanged at an 
arbitrarily poor rate without reverting. WAuraPools.burn only calculates and 
transfers reward amounts; it provides no valuation or slippage protection. The 
UniswapV2Oracle shown is an LP pricing oracle and is not consulted by this 
liquidation path. Although the direct economic impact may depend on who invokes 
the close operation and the resulting debt accounting, the absence of any 
minimum-output bound and reliance on router spot execution clearly establish the
reported vulnerability.


